 by name. Cleaner than echoing and capturing for arrays\u002Fcomplex data.",[1509,1558,1559,1549,1561,1563,1564,1567,1568,1570,1571,1574,1575,161],{},[357,1560,1513],{},[18,1562,1471],{}," for cleanup — ",[18,1565,1566],{},"trap cleanup EXIT INT TERM"," runs ",[18,1569,1423],{}," on exit, Ctrl-C, or kill. Use to remove temp files, release locks. ",[18,1572,1573],{},"EXIT"," runs on any exit (normal or error with ",[18,1576,1577],{},"set -e",[1509,1579,1580,1582,1583,1586,1587,1590,1591,1594],{},[357,1581,1513],{},": pass arrays by name (nameref) or expand elements — ",[18,1584,1585],{},"func \"${arr[@]}\""," passes elements as separate args; ",[18,1588,1589],{},"local -n ref=$1; func arr"," passes by name. You can't pass an array directly (",[18,1592,1593],{},"func $arr"," loses elements).",[31,1596,1598],{"id":1597},"️-edge-cases-gotchas","⚠️ Edge Cases & Gotchas",[1506,1600,1601,1621,1648,1668,1683,1697,1715,1736,1751,1762],{},[1509,1602,1603,1608,1609,1612,1613,1616,1617,1620],{},[357,1604,1605,1607],{},[18,1606,470],{}," is exit status (0-255), not a value",": ",[18,1610,1611],{},"return 5"," sets the exit status to 5 (not \"returns 5\"). For data, echo to stdout and capture with ",[18,1614,1615],{},"$(func)",". ",[18,1618,1619],{},"return 300"," wraps to 44 (modulo 256).",[1509,1622,1623,1628,1629,1632,1633,1635,1636,1638,1639,1641,1642,1644,1645,1647],{},[357,1624,1625,1627],{},[18,1626,20],{}," is dynamic-scoped",": a ",[18,1630,1631],{},"local x"," in ",[18,1634,932],{}," is visible in ",[18,1637,964],{}," (called by ",[18,1640,932],{},"), unless ",[18,1643,964],{}," also declares ",[18,1646,1631],{},". Not lexical — surprising for developers from other languages.",[1509,1649,1650,1656,1657,25,1659,25,1661,1663,1664,1667],{},[357,1651,1652,1653,1655],{},"Functions don't see the script's ",[18,1654,24],{}," by default",": a function has its own ",[18,1658,24],{},[18,1660,194],{},[18,1662,28],{},". If the function needs the script's args, pass them explicitly (",[18,1665,1666],{},"func \"$@\"",") or use a global.",[1509,1669,1670,1675,1676,1678,1679,1682],{},[357,1671,1672,1674],{},[18,1673,347],{}," in a function is the script name, not the function",": in most shells, ",[18,1677,347],{}," doesn't change inside a function. To get the function name, use ",[18,1680,1681],{},"${FUNCNAME[0]}"," (Bash).",[1509,1684,1685,1688,1689,1692,1693,1696],{},[357,1686,1687],{},"Recursion is slow",": each ",[18,1690,1691],{},"$(func ...)"," is a subprocess. ",[18,1694,1695],{},"fib 20"," is slow (thousands of subprocesss). Use iterative algorithms or a different language for heavy computation.",[1509,1698,1699,1704,1705,1708,1709,1712,1713,617],{},[357,1700,1701,1703],{},[18,1702,1083],{}," for child processes",": functions aren't inherited by child processes (subshells yes, child processes no). ",[18,1706,1707],{},"export -f func"," exports it. Needed for ",[18,1710,1711],{},"find ... -exec func {} \\;"," or ",[18,1714,1087],{},[1509,1716,1717,1608,1722,1725,1726,1729,1730,1732,1733,1735],{},[357,1718,1719,1721],{},[18,1720,20],{}," doesn't apply to special variables",[18,1723,1724],{},"local PATH=..."," works, but ",[18,1727,1728],{},"local $?"," doesn't (special). Also ",[18,1731,20],{}," on a nameref (",[18,1734,663],{},") is Bash 4.3+.",[1509,1737,1738,1608,1741,1744,1745,1747,1748,1750],{},[357,1739,1740],{},"Nameref name collision",[18,1742,1743],{},"local -n arr=$1"," — if ",[18,1746,24],{}," is ",[18,1749,1263],{}," (same name), it creates a circular reference. Use a distinct local name or a prefix.",[1509,1752,1753,1756,1757,1761],{},[357,1754,1755],{},"Function defined after use fails",": Bash reads the script top-to-bottom. A function must be defined ",[1758,1759,1760],"em",{},"before"," it's called. Forward references don't work (unlike Python\u002FJS hoisting).",[1509,1763,1764,1770,1771,1774,1775,1777],{},[357,1765,1766,1769],{},[18,1767,1768],{},"trap EXIT"," runs on any exit",": including ",[18,1772,1773],{},"exit",", normal end, and ",[18,1776,1577],{}," abort. It runs in the exiting shell. Order: the exit-triggering command's trap, then EXIT.",[31,1779,1781],{"id":1780},"spot-the-bug","🧠 Spot the Bug",[14,1783,1784],{},"A developer writes a function to increment a counter, but the counter keeps resetting:",[36,1786,1787],{"language":38},[40,1788,1790],{"className":42,"code":1789,"language":38,"meta":44,"style":44},"increment() {\n    count=$((count + 1))\n    echo \"$count\"\n}\n\nincrement   # 1\nincrement   # 1 (expected 2!)\n",[18,1791,1792,1799,1819,1829,1833,1837,1843],{"__ignoreMap":44},[48,1793,1794,1797],{"class":50,"line":51},[48,1795,1796],{"class":61},"increment",[48,1798,66],{"class":65},[48,1800,1801,1804,1806,1809,1812,1815,1817],{"class":50,"line":58},[48,1802,1803],{"class":65},"    count",[48,1805,245],{"class":107},[48,1807,1808],{"class":65},"$((",[48,1810,1811],{"class":61},"count",[48,1813,1814],{"class":76}," +",[48,1816,320],{"class":72},[48,1818,710],{"class":65},[48,1820,1821,1823,1825,1827],{"class":50,"line":69},[48,1822,73],{"class":72},[48,1824,262],{"class":76},[48,1826,719],{"class":65},[48,1828,302],{"class":76},[48,1830,1831],{"class":50,"line":85},[48,1832,88],{"class":65},[48,1834,1835],{"class":50,"line":91},[48,1836,95],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":94},[48,1838,1839,1841],{"class":50,"line":98},[48,1840,1796],{"class":61},[48,1842,736],{"class":54},[48,1844,1845,1847],{"class":50,"line":104},[48,1846,1796],{"class":61},[48,1848,1849],{"class":54},"   # 1 (expected 2!)\n",[14,1851,1852,1853,1855],{},"Why does ",[18,1854,1811],{}," reset each call?",[1857,1858,1859,1863,1883,1900,1912,1919,1973,1982,1987,2060,2066],"details",{},[1860,1861,1862],"summary",{},"Answer",[14,1864,1865,1868,1869,1871,1872,1874,1875,1877,1878,25,1880,1882],{},[18,1866,1867],{},"count=$((count + 1))"," creates a ",[357,1870,20],{}," ",[18,1873,1811],{}," (via ",[18,1876,20],{},"-like behavior in the arithmetic context? No — actually, without ",[18,1879,20],{},[18,1881,1811],{}," is global). Wait, let me reconsider.",[14,1884,1885,1886,1888,1889,1891,1892,1895,1896,1899],{},"Actually, ",[18,1887,1867],{}," without ",[18,1890,20],{}," is a ",[357,1893,1894],{},"global"," assignment. So the first call sets global ",[18,1897,1898],{},"count=1",", the second should set it to 2...",[14,1901,1902,1903,1905,1906,1908,1909,1911],{},"Hmm, the bug might be different. If the script runs each ",[18,1904,1796],{}," in a subshell (e.g., ",[18,1907,1796],{}," is called in a pipeline), the global assignment is lost (subshell). But in the example, ",[18,1910,1796],{}," is called directly (not in a pipeline), so the global should persist.",[14,1913,1914,1915,1918],{},"Let me reconsider the actual common bug: if the function uses ",[18,1916,1917],{},"local count=0"," first, then increments:",[40,1920,1922],{"className":42,"code":1921,"language":38,"meta":44,"style":44},"increment() {\n    local count=0          # reset to 0 each call!\n    count=$((count + 1))\n    echo \"$count\"\n}\n",[18,1923,1924,1930,1943,1959,1969],{"__ignoreMap":44},[48,1925,1926,1928],{"class":50,"line":51},[48,1927,1796],{"class":61},[48,1929,66],{"class":65},[48,1931,1932,1934,1936,1938,1940],{"class":50,"line":58},[48,1933,239],{"class":107},[48,1935,691],{"class":65},[48,1937,245],{"class":107},[48,1939,696],{"class":72},[48,1941,1942],{"class":54},"          # reset to 0 each call!\n",[48,1944,1945,1947,1949,1951,1953,1955,1957],{"class":50,"line":69},[48,1946,1803],{"class":65},[48,1948,245],{"class":107},[48,1950,1808],{"class":65},[48,1952,1811],{"class":61},[48,1954,1814],{"class":76},[48,1956,320],{"class":72},[48,1958,710],{"class":65},[48,1960,1961,1963,1965,1967],{"class":50,"line":85},[48,1962,73],{"class":72},[48,1964,262],{"class":76},[48,1966,719],{"class":65},[48,1968,302],{"class":76},[48,1970,1971],{"class":50,"line":91},[48,1972,88],{"class":65},[14,1974,1975,1976,1978,1979,1981],{},"::\nWith ",[18,1977,1917],{},", each call resets ",[18,1980,1811],{}," to 0, then increments to 1. The counter never goes above 1.",[14,1983,1984,1985,487],{},"The fix — initialize the global once (outside the function), and don't use ",[18,1986,20],{},[40,1988,1990],{"className":42,"code":1989,"language":38,"meta":44,"style":44},"count=0   # global, initialized once\nincrement() {\n    count=$((count + 1))   # no local — modifies the global\n    echo \"$count\"\n}\n\nincrement   # 1\nincrement   # 2\n",[18,1991,1992,2003,2009,2029,2039,2043,2047,2053],{"__ignoreMap":44},[48,1993,1994,1996,1998,2000],{"class":50,"line":51},[48,1995,1811],{"class":65},[48,1997,245],{"class":107},[48,1999,696],{"class":76},[48,2001,2002],{"class":54},"   # global, initialized once\n",[48,2004,2005,2007],{"class":50,"line":58},[48,2006,1796],{"class":61},[48,2008,66],{"class":65},[48,2010,2011,2013,2015,2017,2019,2021,2023,2026],{"class":50,"line":69},[48,2012,1803],{"class":65},[48,2014,245],{"class":107},[48,2016,1808],{"class":65},[48,2018,1811],{"class":61},[48,2020,1814],{"class":76},[48,2022,320],{"class":72},[48,2024,2025],{"class":65},"))   ",[48,2027,2028],{"class":54},"# no local — modifies the global\n",[48,2030,2031,2033,2035,2037],{"class":50,"line":85},[48,2032,73],{"class":72},[48,2034,262],{"class":76},[48,2036,719],{"class":65},[48,2038,302],{"class":76},[48,2040,2041],{"class":50,"line":91},[48,2042,88],{"class":65},[48,2044,2045],{"class":50,"line":98},[48,2046,95],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":94},[48,2048,2049,2051],{"class":50,"line":104},[48,2050,1796],{"class":61},[48,2052,736],{"class":54},[48,2054,2055,2057],{"class":50,"line":116},[48,2056,1796],{"class":61},[48,2058,2059],{"class":54},"   # 2\n",[14,2061,2062,2063,2065],{},"::\nOr, if you want the counter encapsulated, use a file or a nameref pattern. The key is: don't re-initialize with ",[18,2064,20],{}," inside the function if you want the value to persist.",[14,2067,2068,1608,2071,2073,2074,2076,2077,2079,2080,2082],{},[357,2069,2070],{},"The lesson",[18,2072,1917],{}," inside a function resets ",[18,2075,1811],{}," to 0 on each call (local scope). For a persistent counter, use a global (initialized outside the function) and don't ",[18,2078,20],{}," it. Or use a file\u002Fexternal state. Understand ",[18,2081,20],{}," scope vs global persistence.",[31,2084,2085],{"id":1860},"Summary",[14,2087,2088,2089,2092,2093,25,2095,2097,2098,2101,2102,2104,2105,2107,2108,2110,2111,2113,2114,2116,2117,2119],{},"You can define functions (",[18,2090,2091],{},"name() { ... }","), use arguments (",[18,2094,24],{},[18,2096,28],{},"), return exit status (",[18,2099,2100],{},"return 0\u002F1",") or data (echo + ",[18,2103,486],{},"), use ",[18,2106,20],{}," (always!), namerefs (",[18,2109,663],{},", Bash 4.3+), dynamic scoping, recursion (slow), export functions (",[18,2112,1083],{},"), pass arrays (by name or expand), and trap signals for cleanup — with the ",[18,2115,20],{},"-resets-on-call and ",[18,2118,470],{},"-is-exit-status traps internalized. Next: text processing tools.",[2121,2122,2123],"style",{},"html pre.shiki code .sdCPZ, html code.shiki .sdCPZ{--shiki-default:#6A737D;--shiki-github-dark:#6A737D}html pre.shiki code .sIsaT, html code.shiki .sIsaT{--shiki-default:#6F42C1;--shiki-github-dark:#B392F0}html pre.shiki code .ssxIu, html code.shiki .ssxIu{--shiki-default:#24292E;--shiki-github-dark:#E1E4E8}html pre.shiki code .snvgF, html code.shiki .snvgF{--shiki-default:#005CC5;--shiki-github-dark:#79B8FF}html pre.shiki code .sJ6F3, html code.shiki .sJ6F3{--shiki-default:#032F62;--shiki-github-dark:#9ECBFF}html pre.shiki code .svdQ7, html code.shiki .svdQ7{--shiki-default:#D73A49;--shiki-github-dark:#F97583}html .default .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-default-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-default-text-decoration);}html .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font